      <h1>DBA vs Trademark: What Really Protects a Business Name</h1>
      <div class="entry-content">
<p>A DBA, an LLC name and a domain each let you operate under a name. None gives you the right to stop a competitor. A DBA is a county filing. A trademark is a right to stop others using a confusingly similar brand, and it comes from use in commerce, with or without a federal registration.</p>
<p>The longer version matters, because founders spend money in the wrong order. Pick a name, buy the domain, form the LLC, order signs, file a DBA because the bank asked. Nobody checked whether the name was available to own.</p>
<p>So the dba vs trademark question is not academic. One filing is paperwork. The other is a right you can enforce.</p>
<h2 id="dba-vs-trademark-four-names-five-levels-of-protection">DBA vs trademark: four names, five levels of protection</h2>
<p>A California business can hold four name-related things at once, and they overlap just enough to confuse people.</p>
<h3 id="the-legal-entity-name">The legal entity name</h3>
<p>Forming an LLC or corporation registers a name with the <a href="https://www.sos.ca.gov/business-programs/business-entities">California Secretary of State</a>. The state runs one check. Is the proposed name distinguishable from entity names already in its records? That is the test it runs.</p>
<p>It is narrow by design. The Secretary of State does not search trademarks, county fictitious business name indexes, or unregistered businesses. You can reserve a name for a short window while you organize, but a reservation is a place in line. Not ownership.</p>
<p>Filing articles of incorporation does create a real legal advantage. Under <a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=BPC&amp;sectionNum=14415">Business and Professions Code section 14415</a>, a corporation that files first and actually trades under its corporate name gets a statewide rebuttable presumption that it has the exclusive right to use that name, and any confusingly similar name. It is a presumption rather than a registration right, and a competitor can rebut it, but it is more than nothing.</p>
<p>If you are picking a name you intend to enforce, our <a href="/intellectual-property-lawyer-san-francisco/">intellectual property practice</a> starts with clearance, not filing.</p>
<h3 id="the-fictitious-business-name-california-s-dba">The fictitious business name: California's DBA</h3>
<p>California calls it a fictitious business name. The statement goes to the county clerk where the business has its principal place of business. Not the Secretary of State, and there is no statewide DBA registry here. Anyone who says otherwise is quoting another state's rules. One exception: a business with no California location files with the Clerk of Sacramento County. That is a venue rule, not a statewide registry.</p>
<p>The filing exists so the public can find out who owns a business. It is a consumer protection record. And it does not reserve the name. A clerk will take the same name from two registrants in the same week, and will not reject a filing because someone got there first.</p>
<p>There is one real advantage, and it is narrower than it sounds. Under <a href="https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=BPC&amp;sectionNum=14411">Business and Professions Code section 14411</a>, filing a fictitious business name statement creates a rebuttable presumption of the exclusive right to use that name, but only in the county where you filed it. Presumption. One county. Rebuttable. A federal registration adds nationwide rights and a presumption of validity, but it sits alongside state trade-name law rather than replacing it. If another business holds a state-level presumption, that argument still has to be answered.</p>
<p>Filing also triggers publication: four consecutive weekly notices in a newspaper of general circulation, with proof back to the clerk. Skip it and the registration is incomplete, which can block you from suing on a contract signed under the name.</p>
<h3 id="the-domain-name">The domain name</h3>
<p>A domain is a lease on an address. You pay a registrar, you control it while you keep paying, and you can lose it to non-renewal or a dispute. What you do not get is any right to the brand.</p>
<p>Registering a name as a domain does not stop anyone trading under it, and creates no priority over a company already selling under the name. In a fight, the domain is usually the thing at risk.</p>
<h3 id="the-trademark">The trademark</h3>
<p>A trademark is what customers use to tell your goods or services from someone else's. Rights start with use in commerce. Federal registration sharpens them: nationwide constructive notice, a presumption of ownership, Customs recordation, and standing in federal court.</p>
<p>Registration is a process, not a purchase. You file, an examiner reviews, the mark publishes for opposition, and registration issues if nobody objects. The USPTO explains <a href="https://www.uspto.gov/trademarks/basics">how the process works</a> and handles filings through its <a href="https://www.uspto.gov/trademarks/apply">online application system</a>.</p>
<p>One limit worth knowing. A trademark covers a brand identifier, not the content of your work. If you are unsure which protection fits, <a href="/blog/trademark-vs-copyright-whats-the-difference/">trademark versus copyright</a> sorts out the split.</p>
<h2 id="what-each-layer-actually-protects">What each layer actually protects</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Protection</th><th>What it covers</th><th>What it does not cover</th><th>Where it is obtained</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal entity name</td><td>Your name on state records; the name contracts and accounts sit under; a statewide rebuttable presumption of exclusive use if the corporation filed first and trades under the name</td><td>No protection for a product or brand name unless it is also your corporate name</td><td>California Secretary of State</td></tr><tr><td>Fictitious business name (DBA)</td><td>Operating and banking under a different name; a public record of the owner</td><td>Ownership. Nothing beyond a county presumption</td><td>County clerk, plus newspaper publication</td></tr><tr><td>Domain name</td><td>That web address, while you keep paying</td><td>Any brand rights, or priority over earlier users</td><td>A domain registrar</td></tr><tr><td>Common law trademark</td><td>Rights where you actually sold, for what you sold</td><td>Nationwide priority, or a presumption of ownership</td><td>Use in commerce, no filing</td></tr><tr><td>Federal trademark registration</td><td>Nationwide rights for the listed goods and services</td><td>Unlisted classes; fair or descriptive uses</td><td>USPTO</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="why-an-llc-filing-does-not-stop-another-business-using-your-name">Why an LLC filing does not stop another business using your name</h2>
<p>Picture two businesses. One is a registered California LLC, Harbor Line Studio LLC. The other is a sole proprietor down the street trading as Harbor Line Studio with no filings. The LLC has a certificate. The sole proprietor has customers.</p>
<p>The Secretary of State will not intervene. It has no enforcement function over name use. Its database is an index, not a police force.</p>
<p>Formation is not a defense either. If your registered entity name infringes someone's mark, the certificate changes nothing about your exposure. You can be an LLC in good standing and still get a cease and desist letter.</p>
<p>What decides these fights is priority of use in a market, plus registration status. That is why the order of operations below puts a search before formation.</p>
<h2 id="why-a-domain-name-is-an-address-not-a-brand">Why a domain name is an address, not a brand</h2>
<p>Buying example.com feels like claiming the name. It is closer to renting a storefront on a street you do not control.</p>
<p>Say you register a name and build a site. Eight months in, a company with a federal registration for that name in your class sends a letter. Your defense is not "I own the domain." The holder can pursue it through an ICANN-approved arbitration process or in federal court under the Anticybersquatting Consumer Protection Act. Bad faith matters there. It is not a shield.</p>
<p>So buy the domain, knowing what it is: distribution, not protection. If you want to see how enforcement works when the mark is already yours, <a href="/blog/what-should-i-do-if-someone-uses-my-trademark-without-permission/">what to do if someone uses your trademark without permission</a> covers the first steps.</p>
<h2 id="california-where-each-filing-goes">California: where each filing goes</h2>
<p>None of these offices talk to each other, which is the root of most of the confusion.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Entity name.</strong> California Secretary of State, tested only for distinguishability on state records.</li><li><strong>Fictitious business name.</strong> County clerk of the principal place of business, plus four weeks of newspaper publication. Other counties can be added once the home county filing is done.</li><li><strong>Federal trademark.</strong> USPTO, one application per mark, with a filing fee for each class of goods or services, examined for likelihood of confusion.</li><li><strong>California state trademark.</strong> The Secretary of State keeps a state register, but it is a limited tool, and brands that matter commercially go federal.</li></ul>
<p>Practically, a San Francisco founder files the entity with the state, the DBA with the county clerk, and the trademark with the USPTO in Virginia. Three offices, three tests, three answers to "is this name mine?"</p>
<h2 id="when-a-trademark-is-worth-filing">When a trademark is worth filing</h2>
<p>File before launch if the name is the brand and you plan to spend real money on it. Signs, packaging, ads bidding on your own name, a marketplace listing. All of that is money attached to a name you may not own.</p>
<p>The minimum step is clearance, not filing. Start with the <a href="https://www.uspto.gov/trademarks/search">USPTO trademark search</a>, then widen out. The federal database does not show unregistered users, state registrations, or businesses trading under a name with no filing anywhere.</p>
<p>Signals that now is the time:</p>
<ul><li>You are about to spend more on signage than a filing costs.</li><li>Investors or a buyer will ask whether the brand is protected.</li><li>You sell where the brand tools require a registration.</li><li>You have traded for a year and the name has stuck.</li></ul>
<p>Signals to wait: the name is still moving, the mark describes what you sell, or you are pre-revenue and still testing the business.</p>
<p>Waiting is not free either. Rights build from first use, so keep dated evidence of your first sale under the name. Invoices, order confirmations, dated screenshots.</p>
<h2 id="the-order-of-operations-for-a-new-business">The order of operations for a new business</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Shortlist five names, not one.</strong> You need room to walk away.</li><li><strong>Search all three registers the same day.</strong> USPTO, Secretary of State, county index. A clean result in one says nothing about the others.</li><li><strong>Check the domain and the obvious handles</strong> before you fall in love with a name.</li><li><strong>Form the entity</strong> once the name survives step two.</li><li><strong>File the fictitious business name</strong> if you will trade under anything other than the legal entity name, then publish.</li><li><strong>File the trademark</strong> in the classes where you sell or plan to sell.</li><li><strong>Order the signage last.</strong> After the search, not before.</li></ol>
<p>The expensive mistake is not a bad name. It is a good one, built on for two years, that someone else registered first. Then you are the one rebranding.</p>
<h2 id="start-with-the-search-not-the-filing">Start with the search, not the filing</h2>
<p>If you take one thing from this post, take the order. Search before you print. Search before you file the entity. Search before the domain purchase feels like a commitment.</p>
<p>Sulfab Law Office handles trademark clearance and filing for small businesses from the Financial District office, and can look at the entity and DBA questions at the same time. Read about the attorneys on <a href="/meet-our-team/">meet our team</a>.</p>
<p>If you are deciding between filing now and waiting, call (415) 360-5266 and describe the business. A short conversation about classes and timing usually settles it.</p>
<p><em>This article is general information about California business names, not legal advice. Published November 20, 2026.</em></p>
<h2 id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<h3 id="is-a-dba-the-same-as-a-trademark">Is a DBA the same as a trademark?</h3>
<p>No. A DBA lets you operate under a name different from your legal name. It creates no ownership. A trademark is the right to stop others using a confusingly similar brand for similar goods or services. Many businesses need both, for different reasons.</p>
<h3 id="do-i-need-a-trademark-for-my-llc">Do I need a trademark for my LLC?</h3>
<p>Not legally. Forming an LLC is enough to operate. But the LLC filing gives you no right to stop another business using your name, so many businesses that care about their brand file separately. A trademark is what stops them.</p>
<h3 id="can-i-trademark-my-dba-name">Can I trademark my DBA name?</h3>
<p>Yes, if you use it as a brand in commerce. The DBA filing creates no rights, but it does not block you either. You apply to the USPTO like any other applicant, in the classes where you sell. Many owners trademark the name they actually trade under.</p>
<h3 id="does-owning-the-domain-mean-i-own-the-name">Does owning the domain mean I own the name?</h3>
<p>No. A domain registration is a contract with a registrar for an address. It confers no trademark rights and no priority over earlier users. If someone holds a registered mark for the name, they can challenge the domain, and the domain is often what gets lost.</p>
<h3 id="do-i-need-to-file-a-fictitious-business-name-in-california">Do I need to file a fictitious business name in California?</h3>
<p>If you operate for profit under a name that does not include your surname, or one that suggests other owners such as "and Company" or "Associates", generally yes. File with the county clerk where your principal place of business sits; a business with no California location files in Sacramento County. Corporations and LLCs file too when they trade under another name. Publication in a newspaper of general circulation is generally required.</p>
<h3 id="what-if-someone-is-already-using-my-business-name">What if someone is already using my business name?</h3>
<p>Check what kind of use it is. An entity registration on state records does not always block you. A prior user in your market, or a registered mark in your class, is a bigger problem. That is where a clearance opinion usually costs less than a rebrand.</p>
